WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.416
&gt;&gt; It's my great pleasure
to introduce Daniel Selsam.

00:00:07.416 --> 00:00:09.055
Daniel is a student,

00:00:09.056 --> 00:00:11.865
a PhD candidate at Stanford.

00:00:11.866 --> 00:00:15.260
He was interning for
us one and a half

00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:16.400
years ago I have been

00:00:16.401 --> 00:00:18.380
collaborating with him
for like three years,

00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:20.025
in completely different topics.

00:00:20.026 --> 00:00:22.650
Last time he visited
us he was using

00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:25.265
former methods to verify
Machine Learning algorithms.

00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:27.630
And today he is
showing the progress

00:00:27.631 --> 00:00:30.270
he made in trying to
learn a SAT Solver,

00:00:30.271 --> 00:00:34.905
to have a neuro network
that learns SAT.

00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:41.565
&gt;&gt; Thanks Leo. Hi, everybody
thanks for coming today.

00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:44.150
I'll be showing you how
to learn an entire SAT

00:00:44.151 --> 00:00:47.155
Solving Algorithm from
Single-Bit Supervision.

00:00:47.156 --> 00:00:48.690
And this is all joint work with

00:00:48.691 --> 00:00:52.210
Leonardo and several others
back at Stanford.

00:00:52.211 --> 00:00:55.070
So, as most of you know
neural networks have beaten the

00:00:55.071 --> 00:00:57.080
state-of-the-art on
many important problems

00:00:57.081 --> 00:00:58.475
just in the last few years.

00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:01.855
Most notably object recognition
and speech recognition.

00:01:01.856 --> 00:01:04.865
But there have also been
major successes in diverse,

00:01:04.866 --> 00:01:07.085
and I think unexpected domains

00:01:07.086 --> 00:01:10.800
including machine translation,
social network analysis,

00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:13.475
heuristics for board games
like chess and Go,

00:01:13.476 --> 00:01:15.590
even predicting
properties of molecules,

00:01:15.591 --> 00:01:17.695
optimizing data
center efficiency,

00:01:17.696 --> 00:01:19.240
and surprisingly even learning

00:01:19.241 --> 00:01:21.470
high-performance
indexing data structures.

00:01:21.471 --> 00:01:23.740
And yet we still know
very little about

00:01:23.741 --> 00:01:26.310
what neural networks
can and cannot do.

00:01:26.311 --> 00:01:28.980
In this work we consider
the following question,

00:01:28.981 --> 00:01:30.530
can a Neural Network learn to

00:01:30.531 --> 00:01:32.780
perform precise
logical reasoning?

00:01:32.781 --> 00:01:34.420
And more specifically, can

00:01:34.421 --> 00:01:37.220
a neural network learn
to solve SAT problems?

00:01:37.221 --> 00:01:39.280
Here's our setup. We're going

00:01:39.281 --> 00:01:40.510
to train a neural network as

00:01:40.511 --> 00:01:44.130
a classifier on a data set
of input output pairs,

00:01:44.131 --> 00:01:46.300
where the input is
a SAT problem P,

00:01:46.301 --> 00:01:48.350
and the output is a Single-Bit

00:01:48.351 --> 00:01:51.315
indicating whether or
not P is satisfiable.

00:01:51.316 --> 00:01:53.790
At test time we're
going to feed problems

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:56.605
into our trained model
which we call NeuroSAT.

00:01:56.606 --> 00:01:58.880
And it's going to predict
as it was trained to do

00:01:58.881 --> 00:02:01.340
whether the problems
are satisfaible or not.

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:02.855
As we'll see later in the talk,

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:05.000
it's able to predict
with high accuracy

00:02:05.001 --> 00:02:07.415
and when it does
predict satisfiable,

00:02:07.416 --> 00:02:10.445
we can almost always
decode an actual solution,

00:02:10.446 --> 00:02:11.950
a satisfying assignment from

00:02:11.951 --> 00:02:14.670
the internal activations
of the network.

00:02:14.671 --> 00:02:17.085
And we'll see how
this works shortly.

00:02:17.086 --> 00:02:19.190
For some background on SAT,

00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:20.240
I think most of you will be

00:02:20.241 --> 00:02:22.225
familiar with this
but just in case.

00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:23.880
A propositional formula can be

00:02:23.881 --> 00:02:25.975
represented as an AND of ORS.

00:02:25.976 --> 00:02:27.470
So here's an example formula,

00:02:27.471 --> 00:02:29.605
not X one or X two.

00:02:29.606 --> 00:02:33.230
And not X two or X
one, some jargon.

00:02:33.231 --> 00:02:34.600
(X one not X one),

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:37.155
(X two not X two) were
all called literals.

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:40.345
And C one and C two are
both called clauses.

00:02:40.346 --> 00:02:42.775
Now a formula is satisfiable

00:02:42.776 --> 00:02:45.230
if there exists
a satisfying assignment.

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.840
So the formula above is
satisfiable because there

00:02:47.841 --> 00:02:51.215
exists a satisfying
assignment specifically,

00:02:51.216 --> 00:02:53.840
the assignment that maps
X one and X two both to

00:02:53.841 --> 00:02:55.925
true satisfies it because

00:02:55.926 --> 00:02:58.745
the first clause is satisfied
because X two is true.

00:02:58.746 --> 00:03:02.375
And the second clause is
satisfied because X one is true.

00:03:02.376 --> 00:03:04.920
And the SAT problem
is given a formula,

00:03:04.921 --> 00:03:08.070
determine if it is
satisfiable and if it is,

00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:10.125
find a satisfying assignment.

00:03:10.126 --> 00:03:12.580
Now, our goal is to
train a neural network

00:03:12.581 --> 00:03:14.520
to predict satisfiability
and we're going

00:03:14.521 --> 00:03:16.260
to talk about decoding
solutions and actually

00:03:16.261 --> 00:03:18.685
solving the problems later on.

00:03:18.686 --> 00:03:20.950
And there are two main
design challenges,

00:03:20.951 --> 00:03:22.800
what problems do we train on?

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:24.750
And with what kind
of architecture?

00:03:24.751 --> 00:03:27.025
Let's talk about
training data first.

00:03:27.026 --> 00:03:28.470
So, the issue is that

00:03:28.471 --> 00:03:30.180
some problem
distributions might be

00:03:30.181 --> 00:03:32.180
arbitrarily easy to classify,

00:03:32.181 --> 00:03:34.680
based on superficial properties.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.130
But, a network that's

00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:39.670
trained on such a data set
probably will exploit

00:03:39.671 --> 00:03:41.890
those properties and
won't generalize and we

00:03:41.891 --> 00:03:44.505
won't really have learned
anything semantic about SAT.

00:03:44.506 --> 00:03:46.770
So we want to construct
difficult problems to

00:03:46.771 --> 00:03:49.130
force it to learn
something general,

00:03:49.131 --> 00:03:53.420
to be more systematic about
exploring the SAT problem.

00:03:53.421 --> 00:03:56.780
So we define a distribution
that we call SR of N over

00:03:56.781 --> 00:04:00.400
problems such that the problems
come in pairs, one unSAT,

00:04:00.401 --> 00:04:03.430
one SAT, and they differ
by negating a single

00:04:03.431 --> 00:04:04.880
literal and a single clause but

00:04:04.881 --> 00:04:07.490
otherwise have
the same statistics.

00:04:07.491 --> 00:04:10.235
So, the idea is that

00:04:10.236 --> 00:04:13.020
no simple superficial
statistical analysis

00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:15.085
will be able to distinguish
between the two.

00:04:15.086 --> 00:04:17.530
And specifically
to sample a pair of

00:04:17.531 --> 00:04:20.160
problem from SR of N we keep

00:04:20.161 --> 00:04:22.735
sampling uniformly
random clauses

00:04:22.736 --> 00:04:25.230
until the problem
is unsatisfiable.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.940
At which point we flip a single

00:04:26.941 --> 00:04:29.600
literal in the final clause
to make it SAT,

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:31.330
and we return the pair

00:04:31.331 --> 00:04:34.685
before and after we
flip that literal.

00:04:34.686 --> 00:04:37.930
This distribution
is not guaranteed to

00:04:37.931 --> 00:04:40.080
be hard at any complexity
theoretic sense,

00:04:40.081 --> 00:04:42.325
but it's intuitively
statistically hard.

00:04:42.326 --> 00:04:45.060
For small values of
N it's still easy for

00:04:45.061 --> 00:04:46.935
traditional SAT solvers but it

00:04:46.936 --> 00:04:50.195
will be very hard for
a standard classifier.

00:04:50.196 --> 00:04:53.195
Now let's talk about
Network Architecture.

00:04:53.196 --> 00:04:55.910
So we could treat SAT problems
as character strings.

00:04:55.911 --> 00:04:58.910
&gt;&gt; You said this are Clauses?

00:04:58.911 --> 00:05:00.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:00.131 --> 00:05:03.295
&gt;&gt; You keep sampling
random Clauses?

00:05:03.296 --> 00:05:05.010
&gt;&gt; As if they were
learned case you know,

00:05:05.011 --> 00:05:07.580
as if it is uniform
learned on SAT.

00:05:07.581 --> 00:05:10.770
&gt;&gt; And then you add
clauses. You can-

00:05:10.771 --> 00:05:14.930
&gt;&gt; We start with
N variables and no clauses.

00:05:14.931 --> 00:05:15.136
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:05:15.136 --> 00:05:18.520
&gt;&gt; We'll sample
a random uniform clause

00:05:18.521 --> 00:05:20.115
on the N variables,

00:05:20.116 --> 00:05:23.240
and check clause SAT over
to see if it's satisfiable.

00:05:23.241 --> 00:05:23.671
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:23.671 --> 00:05:25.775
&gt;&gt; If it's satisfiable
we do it again.

00:05:25.776 --> 00:05:27.530
We keep adding
clauses like this.

00:05:27.531 --> 00:05:28.590
&gt;&gt; So you keeping adding
clauses, okay that

00:05:28.591 --> 00:05:30.195
wasn't clear here so.

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:31.955
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Sorry keep
sampling random clauses

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:33.550
and adding them until.

00:05:33.551 --> 00:05:35.895
&gt;&gt; And keep adding
them until unSAT.

00:05:35.896 --> 00:05:36.601
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:38.880
&gt;&gt; Sorry that wasn't clear.

00:05:38.881 --> 00:05:40.455
&gt;&gt; The point is that,.

00:05:40.456 --> 00:05:42.665
&gt;&gt; It's going a little fast.

00:05:42.666 --> 00:05:46.030
&gt;&gt;So we know that before

00:05:46.031 --> 00:05:47.080
the last clause is

00:05:47.081 --> 00:05:49.175
introduced the problem
is still satisfiable.

00:05:49.176 --> 00:05:51.120
So we know that
flipping any literal in

00:05:51.121 --> 00:05:53.410
the last clause will preserve

00:05:53.411 --> 00:05:56.565
the model that was
satisfying previously.

00:05:56.566 --> 00:05:57.960
So we know we can recover

00:05:57.961 --> 00:06:00.270
a satisfiable instance by

00:06:00.271 --> 00:06:03.090
flipping a literal in
the final clause yeah.

00:06:03.091 --> 00:06:04.910
Sorry if I'm going
too fast please

00:06:04.911 --> 00:06:06.625
anyone stop me at anytime.

00:06:06.626 --> 00:06:09.080
&gt;&gt; All right I get
it now. Okay thanks.

00:06:09.081 --> 00:06:12.000
&gt;&gt; So, let's talk about
Network Architecture.

00:06:12.001 --> 00:06:14.000
We could treat SAT problems as

00:06:14.001 --> 00:06:16.745
character strings and use
standard sequence models.

00:06:16.746 --> 00:06:19.710
We saw that, I wrote
a SAT problem above it's

00:06:19.711 --> 00:06:23.600
characters so the AST standard
models can be applied.

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:26.260
But there are many invariances
that we want to enforce

00:06:26.261 --> 00:06:29.955
that the logic of
satisfiability admits.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:31.580
For example, SAT problems are

00:06:31.581 --> 00:06:33.710
permutation invariant
in a few ways.

00:06:33.711 --> 00:06:34.920
You can permute literals,

00:06:34.921 --> 00:06:36.210
you can permute
clauses and you can

00:06:36.211 --> 00:06:37.910
permute literals
within a clause.

00:06:37.911 --> 00:06:39.970
And you don't want to
have a Sequence Model

00:06:39.971 --> 00:06:42.095
have to learn all of
this from scratch.

00:06:42.096 --> 00:06:44.030
We can just build
it into the model.

00:06:44.031 --> 00:06:46.195
There are also
negation invariant.

00:06:46.196 --> 00:06:48.180
Which means that you can
negate every occurrence

00:06:48.181 --> 00:06:49.600
of any specific variable,

00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:51.795
also without affecting
satisfiability.

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:53.010
So we're going to try to build

00:06:53.011 --> 00:06:55.870
these invariances into
our Neural Network Model.

00:06:55.871 --> 00:06:59.920
And the approach is given
a formula like this.

00:06:59.921 --> 00:07:03.080
We're going to construct
an undirected graph with

00:07:03.081 --> 00:07:04.850
a node for every literal so

00:07:04.851 --> 00:07:06.580
there's a node for (X1 not X1),

00:07:06.581 --> 00:07:11.080
(X2 not X2) and a node for
every clause C1 and C2.

00:07:11.081 --> 00:07:13.350
There's going to be
one type of edge between

00:07:13.351 --> 00:07:15.685
every literal and
every clause it appears in.

00:07:15.686 --> 00:07:18.660
So there's an edge between
X1 and C1 and X2 and

00:07:18.661 --> 00:07:22.725
C1 because C1 Contains
the literals X1 and X2.

00:07:22.726 --> 00:07:24.730
And there's also a separate type

00:07:24.731 --> 00:07:25.770
of Edge which I did note with

00:07:25.771 --> 00:07:28.875
a dotted edge here between a
literal and its complement.

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:33.540
So X2 and not X2 are
connected by the dotted edge,

00:07:33.541 --> 00:07:36.790
and X1 and not X1 are
connected by that edge.

00:07:36.791 --> 00:07:40.245
Now the basic idea of the model
which we call NeuroSAT,

00:07:40.246 --> 00:07:43.270
is we're going to maintain
a vector embedding

00:07:43.271 --> 00:07:45.640
some inscrutable collection of

00:07:45.641 --> 00:07:48.680
floating point numbers at
each node in the graph.

00:07:48.681 --> 00:07:50.660
And we're going to
iterativly of we pass

00:07:50.661 --> 00:07:52.890
messages along
the edges of the graph.

00:07:52.891 --> 00:07:55.680
So, a literal is going
to pass messages to

00:07:55.681 --> 00:07:59.160
the clauses it appears in
and its complement literal.

00:07:59.161 --> 00:08:00.540
Clauses are going
to pass messages

00:08:00.541 --> 00:08:03.260
back to the literals
that occur in them.

00:08:03.261 --> 00:08:06.460
After teatime steps, we're
going to map literals

00:08:06.461 --> 00:08:09.595
into scalar votes using
a learned network.

00:08:09.596 --> 00:08:10.860
And then we're just
going to average

00:08:10.861 --> 00:08:12.695
the votes to compute
a single logic

00:08:12.696 --> 00:08:14.190
that's going to guess
whether the entire

00:08:14.191 --> 00:08:16.070
problem is satisfiable or not.

00:08:16.071 --> 00:08:17.950
Now I'm going to go
into the architecture

00:08:17.951 --> 00:08:19.950
in great detail in
the next few slides.

00:08:19.951 --> 00:08:20.411
But if you have

00:08:20.411 --> 00:08:21.690
any high level questions
I'll take them now.

00:08:21.691 --> 00:08:22.660
Yeah.

00:08:22.661 --> 00:08:25.480
&gt;&gt;It's related to survey-

00:08:25.481 --> 00:08:26.740
&gt;&gt;I'm going to discuss

00:08:26.741 --> 00:08:28.480
every propagation a
little at the end.

00:08:28.481 --> 00:08:31.805
Tree propagation does
not look like this.

00:08:31.806 --> 00:08:35.395
There are several
architectural differences.

00:08:35.396 --> 00:08:37.635
We'll cover Survey
Propagation with this.

00:08:37.636 --> 00:08:40.060
In Survey Propagation
the messages

00:08:40.061 --> 00:08:42.210
that the clauses
sends to the literal,

00:08:42.211 --> 00:08:44.285
depends on which literal it is.

00:08:44.286 --> 00:08:46.630
Like you marginalize
out the other literals,

00:08:46.631 --> 00:08:48.400
which you can't
express in this way.

00:08:48.401 --> 00:08:50.830
But it's structurally similar

00:08:50.831 --> 00:08:54.760
in the sense that it's
a message-passing algorithm.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.625
So, here's the NeuroSAT model
in more detail.

00:09:04.626 --> 00:09:07.815
Where we have a bunch
of learned parameters.

00:09:07.816 --> 00:09:11.360
So, two vectors, the
L init and C init

00:09:11.361 --> 00:09:13.120
to initialize
the embeddings for each

00:09:13.121 --> 00:09:15.290
of the literals and clauses.

00:09:15.291 --> 00:09:17.610
Three multi-layer
preceptrons, L msg,

00:09:17.611 --> 00:09:18.820
C msg and L vote.

00:09:18.821 --> 00:09:20.660
I'll explain all
of these shortly,

00:09:20.661 --> 00:09:22.860
and then two layer-norm LSTMs,

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:24.585
L update and C update.

00:09:24.586 --> 00:09:26.695
Now, we're going to initialize,

00:09:26.696 --> 00:09:29.520
one matrix that stores all
of the literal embeddings.

00:09:29.521 --> 00:09:30.840
So, it's 2N by D,

00:09:30.841 --> 00:09:33.820
so every row contains

00:09:33.821 --> 00:09:35.640
the embedding for
one of the literals.

00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:37.340
And we're going to
just initialize all

00:09:37.341 --> 00:09:38.860
of the rows to L init,

00:09:38.861 --> 00:09:41.765
the learned vector and
similarly for clauses.

00:09:41.766 --> 00:09:43.485
Every row of C

00:09:43.486 --> 00:09:46.340
is going to be the embedding
for one of the clauses.

00:09:46.341 --> 00:09:49.260
And we're going to run
T iterations of message passing.

00:09:49.261 --> 00:09:50.360
So, every iteration of

00:09:50.361 --> 00:09:52.350
message passing
consists of two stages.

00:09:52.351 --> 00:09:53.780
Stage one, literals are

00:09:53.781 --> 00:09:55.855
going to pass messages
to the clauses.

00:09:55.856 --> 00:09:59.360
So, here all of
the literals have

00:09:59.361 --> 00:10:00.850
embeddings and
they're going to send

00:10:00.851 --> 00:10:03.295
them to the clauses
that they occur in.

00:10:03.296 --> 00:10:07.440
The clauses are going to sum
the incoming messages and

00:10:07.441 --> 00:10:09.360
also get its previous state with

00:10:09.361 --> 00:10:12.145
a started edge and use the rnn,

00:10:12.146 --> 00:10:16.920
the C update layer-norm LSTM
to update its own embedding.

00:10:16.921 --> 00:10:19.060
And here's the matrix notation.

00:10:19.061 --> 00:10:22.535
It's hard to read but it
just says what I just said.

00:10:22.536 --> 00:10:24.045
You've got the
literal embeddings.

00:10:24.046 --> 00:10:25.695
You apply this learned

00:10:25.696 --> 00:10:29.705
MLP to compute the messages,
one for each literal.

00:10:29.706 --> 00:10:32.825
M is going to be
the binary adjacency matrix.

00:10:32.826 --> 00:10:34.780
So, this is just summing

00:10:34.781 --> 00:10:37.650
the messages and sending
them to the clauses and

00:10:37.651 --> 00:10:39.945
then the clause also get

00:10:39.946 --> 00:10:44.340
its previous hidden state
and it calls the update LSTM.

00:10:44.341 --> 00:10:46.905
Now, the stage 2 is similar.

00:10:46.906 --> 00:10:49.165
Clauses pass messages
to the literals.

00:10:49.166 --> 00:10:50.880
The only difference here is that

00:10:50.881 --> 00:10:54.010
the literals get messages
from the clauses,

00:10:54.011 --> 00:10:55.435
it gets its old state

00:10:55.436 --> 00:10:57.060
and it also gets
a different kind of

00:10:57.061 --> 00:10:58.640
message from its complement

00:10:58.641 --> 00:11:00.585
because of the extra edges
in the graph.

00:11:00.586 --> 00:11:03.940
Intuitively, these dotted edges
encode the constraint

00:11:03.941 --> 00:11:07.435
that they should probably
not be equal. Yes.

00:11:07.436 --> 00:11:11.245
&gt;&gt; What is a message?
What are the messages?

00:11:11.246 --> 00:11:15.830
&gt;&gt; So, every node has some,

00:11:15.831 --> 00:11:17.300
at every time step
that every node has

00:11:17.301 --> 00:11:20.170
some collection of
floating point numbers that

00:11:20.171 --> 00:11:21.695
represent something

00:11:21.696 --> 00:11:24.980
potentially inscrutable
about that node.

00:11:24.981 --> 00:11:28.420
So, at every time step I
have some vector here which

00:11:28.421 --> 00:11:29.525
represents something

00:11:29.526 --> 00:11:31.950
hopefully meaningful
about the literal.

00:11:31.951 --> 00:11:37.970
Now, that's represented in
this matrix L of t. L msg is

00:11:37.971 --> 00:11:41.470
a learned Neural Network that
takes those embeddings and

00:11:41.471 --> 00:11:43.730
computes some other vector

00:11:43.731 --> 00:11:46.040
of floats that
represents a message.

00:11:46.041 --> 00:11:47.950
But I have no a
priori knowledge of

00:11:47.951 --> 00:11:50.555
what that actually
encodes semantically.

00:11:50.556 --> 00:11:53.970
It has the freedom to choose
what those floats represent.

00:11:53.971 --> 00:11:56.895
&gt;&gt; And what are these sizes
of the spectrum, again?

00:11:56.896 --> 00:11:59.340
&gt;&gt; I don't think I said yet.
We eventually run it with

00:11:59.341 --> 00:12:01.890
128 but is pretty arbitrary.

00:12:01.891 --> 00:12:03.180
&gt;&gt; It's a good number.

00:12:03.181 --> 00:12:04.970
&gt;&gt; If we had bigger
hardware we'd run it.

00:12:04.971 --> 00:12:06.755
&gt;&gt; Got it. Okay, super.

00:12:06.756 --> 00:12:08.640
&gt;&gt; As big as you can get.

00:12:08.641 --> 00:12:12.440
&gt;&gt; It's related to
problem size potentially.

00:12:12.441 --> 00:12:14.775
&gt;&gt; Potentially,
unclear actually.

00:12:14.776 --> 00:12:15.450
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay, okay.

00:12:15.451 --> 00:12:19.440
&gt;&gt; Because reduction sometimes
[inaudible] you don't always want.

00:12:19.441 --> 00:12:23.165
I mean having a smaller vector

00:12:23.166 --> 00:12:25.970
potentially versus
something distractive.

00:12:25.971 --> 00:12:29.030
&gt;&gt; Conventional wisdom in
machine learning is used

00:12:29.031 --> 00:12:31.030
as big a vector as
you can get away

00:12:31.031 --> 00:12:33.800
with in terms of
hardware and performance.

00:12:33.801 --> 00:12:36.540
We also found that it trains,

00:12:36.541 --> 00:12:39.435
the bigger we made D
the faster it trained.

00:12:39.436 --> 00:12:41.830
But it's not major. You
can train these with

00:12:41.831 --> 00:12:43.725
16 even with eight first.

00:12:43.726 --> 00:12:44.625
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:12:44.626 --> 00:12:44.871
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:44.871 --> 00:12:46.185
&gt;&gt; And you're going
to describe how you

00:12:46.186 --> 00:12:47.870
do the learning itself later?

00:12:47.871 --> 00:12:50.725
&gt;&gt; Yeah, although
the short answer is background.

00:12:50.726 --> 00:12:53.660
But I'll explain
the signal in a second.

00:12:53.661 --> 00:12:56.945
So, here's the literals
and it's similar,

00:12:56.946 --> 00:12:58.250
I won't go through
this in detail.

00:12:58.251 --> 00:13:00.590
It's identical except for this,

00:13:00.591 --> 00:13:02.210
except for getting
the compliment of

00:13:02.211 --> 00:13:03.650
the literals as
a separate message

00:13:03.651 --> 00:13:05.140
is the same as the clauses.

00:13:05.141 --> 00:13:07.190
Now, after T iterations,

00:13:07.191 --> 00:13:09.270
we're going to compute
a scalar "vote" for each

00:13:09.271 --> 00:13:10.910
literal by applying

00:13:10.911 --> 00:13:13.170
this learned multi-layer
perceptron on L vote,

00:13:13.171 --> 00:13:16.665
that maps each literal
embedding into a single scalar.

00:13:16.666 --> 00:13:19.650
We're going to then
average the votes to get

00:13:19.651 --> 00:13:21.430
a logit which is
just a single scalar

00:13:21.431 --> 00:13:23.235
for the entire network.

00:13:23.236 --> 00:13:25.805
And then we're going to
predict satisfiability

00:13:25.806 --> 00:13:28.455
using the logit by just
taking the sigmoid function,

00:13:28.456 --> 00:13:30.705
which is going to return
something between zero and one.

00:13:30.706 --> 00:13:33.730
Now, we train with
the cross entropy loss.

00:13:33.731 --> 00:13:36.555
So, we're using
back propagation to minimize

00:13:36.556 --> 00:13:37.890
the classification error on

00:13:37.891 --> 00:13:40.150
a training dataset. Does that-

00:13:40.151 --> 00:13:42.840
&gt;&gt; Where is the input
to your training?

00:13:42.841 --> 00:13:46.865
&gt;&gt; SAT problems
represented as graphs and

00:13:46.866 --> 00:13:48.920
a single bit of supervision
for each problem

00:13:48.921 --> 00:13:51.415
indicating one if it's SAT,

00:13:51.416 --> 00:13:52.980
zero if it's unSAT.

00:13:52.981 --> 00:13:54.440
&gt;&gt; So you use

00:13:54.441 --> 00:13:56.525
these pairs that you
were mentioning earlier.

00:13:56.526 --> 00:13:58.950
Sets of these pairs,
large sets of these pairs.

00:13:58.951 --> 00:14:02.520
&gt;&gt; Yes. Exactly. But typically,

00:14:02.521 --> 00:14:04.170
we don't tell anything
about solutions.

00:14:04.171 --> 00:14:05.520
We're just giving it single

00:14:05.521 --> 00:14:08.075
bit supervision
for each problem.

00:14:08.076 --> 00:14:11.990
Let's talk about
experiments now.

00:14:11.991 --> 00:14:16.285
So, we're going to train
on SR uniform 10, 40.

00:14:16.286 --> 00:14:18.290
The uniform part is
not actually essential.

00:14:18.291 --> 00:14:20.000
It's just intuitively nice

00:14:20.001 --> 00:14:21.250
to use a curriculum here because

00:14:21.251 --> 00:14:22.600
it's going to be
harder to induce

00:14:22.601 --> 00:14:24.885
structure on bigger problems.

00:14:24.886 --> 00:14:26.990
But we train things
without the curriculum

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:28.915
and it works just as
well. And we're going to-

00:14:28.916 --> 00:14:31.870
&gt;&gt; Are those variables
clauses, 10, 40?

00:14:31.871 --> 00:14:33.180
&gt;&gt; No, sorry. Uniform meaning,

00:14:33.181 --> 00:14:35.370
we're sampling the number
of variables from

00:14:35.371 --> 00:14:37.870
a uniform distribution
between 10 and 40.

00:14:37.871 --> 00:14:39.665
The number of
clauses is determined

00:14:39.666 --> 00:14:42.480
by how many we can sample
before it get answered.

00:14:42.481 --> 00:14:42.981
&gt;&gt; Answered, right.

00:14:42.981 --> 00:14:45.180
&gt;&gt; And then we're going
to test on SR(40).

00:14:45.181 --> 00:14:48.870
Just some perspective on SR(40).

00:14:48.871 --> 00:14:50.440
There's 40 variables,
so there are

00:14:50.441 --> 00:14:52.205
a trillion possible assignments.

00:14:52.206 --> 00:14:54.645
About 200 clauses per problem.

00:14:54.646 --> 00:14:57.050
Roughly 1000
literally occurrences,

00:14:57.051 --> 00:14:59.350
so about five literals
per clause.

00:14:59.351 --> 00:15:02.040
And all of the clauses
are uniformly random,

00:15:02.041 --> 00:15:05.035
so every literal is
treated symmetrically.

00:15:05.036 --> 00:15:08.680
Including X I and not X I are
both treated symmetrically.

00:15:08.681 --> 00:15:11.350
And every single problem
in the distribution is a

00:15:11.351 --> 00:15:14.810
single bit away from flipping
between SAT and unSAT.

00:15:14.811 --> 00:15:17.540
And just a caveat,
because 40 is small,

00:15:17.541 --> 00:15:19.670
it is still easy for
state of the results.

00:15:19.671 --> 00:15:21.835
So many SAT can solve SR(40)

00:15:21.836 --> 00:15:26.685
in almost [inaudible] number
of milliseconds.

00:15:26.686 --> 00:15:31.025
And results NeuroSAT predicts
with 85 percent accuracy.

00:15:31.026 --> 00:15:33.610
We're going to make that number
a lot better shortly,

00:15:33.611 --> 00:15:35.125
but first let's
understand how it

00:15:35.126 --> 00:15:37.075
even beat 50 percent. Yes.

00:15:37.076 --> 00:15:39.830
&gt;&gt; So, intuitively
what you learn here,

00:15:39.831 --> 00:15:42.770
is for any pair of formulas,

00:15:42.771 --> 00:15:45.045
whether you have
the original unSAT

00:15:45.046 --> 00:15:47.575
or if there's one flip
which become SAT.

00:15:47.576 --> 00:15:50.905
That's what you are
learning here, correct?

00:15:50.906 --> 00:15:54.960
&gt;&gt; Well, we don't tell
NeuroSAT that these two,

00:15:54.961 --> 00:15:56.155
one is SAT one is unSAT.

00:15:56.156 --> 00:16:00.660
They just get random problems
for distribution.

00:16:00.661 --> 00:16:05.165
&gt;&gt; I see. So, when you
feed for the training,

00:16:05.166 --> 00:16:06.975
you do not say that this one

00:16:06.976 --> 00:16:08.920
was unSAT one and this was SAT?

00:16:08.921 --> 00:16:10.400
&gt;&gt; Correct. We just feed them,

00:16:10.401 --> 00:16:12.335
feed the individual problems.

00:16:12.336 --> 00:16:14.370
&gt;&gt; But they're labeled.

00:16:14.371 --> 00:16:16.370
&gt;&gt; They're labeled but
we don't tell NeuroSAT.

00:16:16.371 --> 00:16:17.715
&gt;&gt; So, you do tell them.

00:16:17.716 --> 00:16:19.000
&gt;&gt; We don't tell NeuroSAT that

00:16:19.001 --> 00:16:20.535
they should look at this pair.

00:16:20.536 --> 00:16:22.710
&gt;&gt; So, input is not a pair.

00:16:22.711 --> 00:16:24.370
&gt;&gt; Every single
individual problem.

00:16:24.371 --> 00:16:26.795
&gt;&gt; No, it's a SAT but he
says it's effectively what.

00:16:26.796 --> 00:16:28.705
Okay, that's not a criticism,

00:16:28.706 --> 00:16:30.390
I'm just trying to see
what the intuition,

00:16:30.391 --> 00:16:32.370
if there's an intuition here.

00:16:32.371 --> 00:16:35.000
What's the intuition that
you had in your head?

00:16:35.001 --> 00:16:37.600
What's actually
been learned here...

00:16:37.601 --> 00:16:39.290
&gt;&gt; We have actually a

00:16:39.291 --> 00:16:40.915
very good explanation
of what's being learned,

00:16:40.916 --> 00:16:42.845
which I'm going
to get to shortly.

00:16:42.846 --> 00:16:43.630
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:16:43.631 --> 00:16:44.950
&gt;&gt; So, we were all really

00:16:44.951 --> 00:16:46.990
surprised by this number
and we trained

00:16:46.991 --> 00:16:48.845
a few baselines including

00:16:48.846 --> 00:16:52.100
LSTMs and we could not break
50 percent on any of them.

00:16:52.101 --> 00:16:53.965
We couldn't train
a model but even

00:16:53.966 --> 00:16:56.090
when you fixed the number
of variables and fixed

00:16:56.091 --> 00:16:58.155
the representation could even

00:16:58.156 --> 00:17:00.380
distinguish on
small training sets,

00:17:00.381 --> 00:17:04.275
the difference between
the SAT and unSAT problems.

00:17:04.276 --> 00:17:06.175
So, the best way,

00:17:06.176 --> 00:17:08.910
we actually bombarded this
with experiments and made

00:17:08.911 --> 00:17:09.980
no progress until we just

00:17:09.981 --> 00:17:12.460
visualized what it was doing
and then it became clear.

00:17:12.461 --> 00:17:14.620
So, this is an example run of

00:17:14.621 --> 00:17:18.570
NeuroSAT on a problem
from SR(20).

00:17:18.571 --> 00:17:21.845
Here, this is the matrix

00:17:21.846 --> 00:17:24.545
of scalar literal votes
at each time step.

00:17:24.546 --> 00:17:27.415
So, this is a 20 by two matrix,

00:17:27.416 --> 00:17:29.235
because there's 20 variables.

00:17:29.236 --> 00:17:31.630
So, the Ith row is going to be

00:17:31.631 --> 00:17:33.840
the scalar vote for X I

00:17:33.841 --> 00:17:36.400
and then not X I in
the second column.

00:17:36.401 --> 00:17:38.120
Here, white is zero,

00:17:38.121 --> 00:17:40.645
red is positive, and
blue is negative.

00:17:40.646 --> 00:17:42.855
Okay? So, red means SAT,

00:17:42.856 --> 00:17:46.095
white means unsure,
blue means unSAT.

00:17:46.096 --> 00:17:47.930
Now, as time goes on,

00:17:47.931 --> 00:17:49.400
there's some red and blue

00:17:49.401 --> 00:17:50.910
here from the
initialization which

00:17:50.911 --> 00:17:54.230
is an artifact of
the initialization vectors.

00:17:54.231 --> 00:17:56.730
But very quickly
all of the literals

00:17:56.731 --> 00:17:59.510
start voting unSAT
with low confidence.

00:17:59.511 --> 00:18:03.000
So, light blue means
roughly logit of negative one.

00:18:03.001 --> 00:18:04.570
So, assigning, say

00:18:04.571 --> 00:18:07.505
16 percent probability
just to it being SAT.

00:18:07.506 --> 00:18:11.325
And you can see there's a lot
happening each time step.

00:18:11.326 --> 00:18:14.030
A lot of the dots are
changing and moving around but

00:18:14.031 --> 00:18:16.050
consistently almost all of

00:18:16.051 --> 00:18:18.350
the literals are voting SAT
with low confidence.

00:18:18.351 --> 00:18:20.785
And we see this on
every problem it runs on.

00:18:20.786 --> 00:18:22.920
And this happens for a while,

00:18:22.921 --> 00:18:24.710
unSAT with no confidence.

00:18:24.711 --> 00:18:26.010
Quite a few things
are happening,

00:18:26.011 --> 00:18:27.590
a few speckles of very,

00:18:27.591 --> 00:18:29.460
very light red that
don't make any difference

00:18:29.461 --> 00:18:31.755
because they get washed
out by the averaging.

00:18:31.756 --> 00:18:33.760
But then, all of a sudden

00:18:33.761 --> 00:18:37.715
there's a very distinct
phase transition.

00:18:37.716 --> 00:18:40.580
All of the literals
start voting SAT with

00:18:40.581 --> 00:18:44.025
extremely high confidence
and the network converges.

00:18:44.026 --> 00:18:47.760
So, these light blues are
about negative one logit.

00:18:47.761 --> 00:18:50.360
These reds are about plus 40.

00:18:50.361 --> 00:18:53.740
So, this is 99.9999999 percent

00:18:53.741 --> 00:18:55.620
sure that these problems

00:18:55.621 --> 00:18:58.060
are satisfiable and
the network convergences.

00:18:58.061 --> 00:19:02.655
We see this for every problem
that it runs on.

00:19:02.656 --> 00:19:05.515
If it's unSAT, it looks
like this forever,

00:19:05.516 --> 00:19:07.710
and if it's SAT it either
looks like this for

00:19:07.711 --> 00:19:14.015
a long time or eventually
end, sometimes, usually,

00:19:14.016 --> 00:19:15.980
eventually has
this phase transition

00:19:15.981 --> 00:19:17.655
where it converges. Yes.

00:19:17.656 --> 00:19:20.250
&gt;&gt; Just to make sure because
that's where we're at.

00:19:20.251 --> 00:19:21.760
This is when you test

00:19:21.761 --> 00:19:23.040
basically, not when
you learn, here. Right?

00:19:23.041 --> 00:19:25.350
&gt;&gt; Yes, this is a problem
that was not trained up.

00:19:25.351 --> 00:19:25.721
&gt;&gt; Okay, okay.

00:19:25.721 --> 00:19:29.220
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, somehow

00:19:29.221 --> 00:19:30.940
around here it becomes extremely

00:19:30.941 --> 00:19:33.720
confident that this problem
is satisfiable.

00:19:33.721 --> 00:19:36.980
Now, we weren't exactly

00:19:36.981 --> 00:19:40.420
sure what it found
that convinced it.

00:19:40.421 --> 00:19:42.390
But if you look carefully at

00:19:42.391 --> 00:19:45.180
the literal votes
after convergence,

00:19:45.181 --> 00:19:48.100
is a little hard to see
because the resolution is poor.

00:19:48.101 --> 00:19:50.200
But you can maybe make out

00:19:50.201 --> 00:19:52.990
a pretty distinct
checkerboard pattern.

00:19:52.991 --> 00:19:55.595
Meaning that there
are only roughly two,

00:19:55.596 --> 00:19:57.720
maybe three shades of red here.

00:19:57.721 --> 00:19:59.530
And for each variable,

00:19:59.531 --> 00:20:01.080
there's one literal that is

00:20:01.081 --> 00:20:03.070
distinctly darker
than the other one.

00:20:03.071 --> 00:20:05.270
So, you can see
a bit pattern here.

00:20:05.271 --> 00:20:08.775
Do you guys all see it roughly?

00:20:08.776 --> 00:20:11.305
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:20:11.306 --> 00:20:13.060
&gt;&gt; You wouldn't call it what?

00:20:13.061 --> 00:20:14.700
&gt;&gt; Sorry, go ahead, go ahead.

00:20:14.701 --> 00:20:16.830
&gt;&gt; So, we hypothesized that

00:20:16.831 --> 00:20:19.485
maybe this is encoding
is satisfying assignment.

00:20:19.486 --> 00:20:20.860
And that it had become so

00:20:20.861 --> 00:20:22.520
confident because
it had found it.

00:20:22.521 --> 00:20:24.585
So when we made
it a script to just

00:20:24.586 --> 00:20:27.280
try to just read

00:20:27.281 --> 00:20:29.010
this off and see if it's
a satisfying assignment,

00:20:29.011 --> 00:20:30.200
and it turns out that it

00:20:30.201 --> 00:20:32.585
usually is a
satisfying assignment.

00:20:32.586 --> 00:20:35.270
Which suggests that the reason

00:20:35.271 --> 00:20:36.860
why I became so
confident is actually

00:20:36.861 --> 00:20:40.760
because it had detected
a satisfying assignment,

00:20:40.761 --> 00:20:42.215
and knew that that indicated

00:20:42.216 --> 00:20:44.260
that the problem
must be satisfiable.

00:20:44.261 --> 00:20:46.250
Now, at first I thought maybe

00:20:46.251 --> 00:20:47.490
the literals that are true in

00:20:47.491 --> 00:20:49.100
the assignment are
voting SAT with

00:20:49.101 --> 00:20:50.480
more confidence even though

00:20:50.481 --> 00:20:51.815
I didn't know why
it would do that,

00:20:51.816 --> 00:20:54.010
it turns out that
is not the case.

00:20:54.011 --> 00:20:57.735
Sometimes, the literal
that is voting lower,

00:20:57.736 --> 00:20:59.865
that is voting SAT
with less confidence,

00:20:59.866 --> 00:21:02.800
is the one that's true
in the solution. Yeah.

00:21:02.801 --> 00:21:03.226
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:21:03.226 --> 00:21:07.660
&gt;&gt; You try both possibilities.

00:21:07.661 --> 00:21:10.680
So, you try the thing
that maps the one

00:21:10.681 --> 00:21:13.800
that's lower to true everywhere,

00:21:13.801 --> 00:21:16.215
and then you try
the one that's- Yeah.

00:21:16.216 --> 00:21:18.120
But this has turned out to be

00:21:18.121 --> 00:21:20.605
an unreliable way to
decode the solutions.

00:21:20.606 --> 00:21:22.190
It turns out that
this pair pattern is

00:21:22.191 --> 00:21:24.920
just an artifact of the fact

00:21:24.921 --> 00:21:28.065
that the higher dimensional
literal embeddings cluster

00:21:28.066 --> 00:21:29.545
according to the assignment.

00:21:29.546 --> 00:21:33.965
So at convergence, in
the 128 dimensional space,

00:21:33.966 --> 00:21:35.250
the literals that are true

00:21:35.251 --> 00:21:36.610
are going to be in one cluster,

00:21:36.611 --> 00:21:38.070
the literals that are
false are going to

00:21:38.071 --> 00:21:39.865
be in another cluster.

00:21:39.866 --> 00:21:41.270
And this pair pattern is just

00:21:41.271 --> 00:21:42.630
an artifact of the projection

00:21:42.631 --> 00:21:45.790
preserving that clustering
most of the time.

00:21:45.791 --> 00:21:49.295
&gt;&gt; Excuse me. If
you have this set,

00:21:49.296 --> 00:21:51.530
you have two [inaudible]
and run it through this.

00:21:51.531 --> 00:21:53.285
And one cluster could be the

00:21:53.286 --> 00:21:55.060
true and the other
could be the false.

00:21:55.061 --> 00:21:58.525
&gt;&gt; Am not.

00:21:58.526 --> 00:22:01.870
&gt;&gt; And suppose that you
have two disjoint sets of

00:22:01.871 --> 00:22:07.625
literals that appear
disjoint sets of clauses,

00:22:07.626 --> 00:22:11.620
then would your learning

00:22:11.621 --> 00:22:14.495
necessarily correlate when is

00:22:14.496 --> 00:22:17.210
light verses dark in
one cluster or the other?

00:22:17.211 --> 00:22:18.370
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:22:18.371 --> 00:22:19.760
In that case, it is

00:22:19.761 --> 00:22:23.080
