rock solid evidence

00:34:01.256 --> 00:34:02.760
that it learns that it needs

00:34:02.761 --> 00:34:06.610
to find somehow a search
for satisfying assignments,

00:34:06.611 --> 00:34:09.765
and that is the best way to
explain the supervision bit.

00:34:09.766 --> 00:34:11.340
&gt;&gt; But how does it find this?

00:34:11.341 --> 00:34:13.620
&gt;&gt; That, I can only speculate.

00:34:13.621 --> 00:34:14.815
&gt;&gt; It's by pattern recognition,

00:34:14.816 --> 00:34:16.310
that's why I'm asking
where you've learned this.

00:34:16.311 --> 00:34:18.485
&gt;&gt; I have one relevant
experiment here.

00:34:18.486 --> 00:34:20.910
Trying to tell whether it
was doing a local search or

00:34:20.911 --> 00:34:23.525
global search. It's not clear.

00:34:23.526 --> 00:34:25.490
So, we can decode
the solution by

00:34:25.491 --> 00:34:28.095
doing this two clustering
technique at the end.

00:34:28.096 --> 00:34:31.245
It's not clear
that if we cluster,

00:34:31.246 --> 00:34:33.535
it's meaningful
before convergence.

00:34:33.536 --> 00:34:35.680
But, we hypothesized
that maybe it

00:34:35.681 --> 00:34:39.160
is an inconsistent assignment
before convergence,

00:34:39.161 --> 00:34:43.320
and we tested what properties
that assignment had.

00:34:43.321 --> 00:34:45.345
So when we do this,

00:34:45.346 --> 00:34:49.080
we see that the number of
clauses satisfied increased

00:34:49.081 --> 00:34:53.435
monotonically very quickly
and rarely go down,

00:34:53.436 --> 00:34:54.690
and we see that the number

00:34:54.691 --> 00:34:55.740
of literals that are flipped at

00:34:55.741 --> 00:34:57.800
each time step starts out high,

00:34:57.801 --> 00:34:59.855
and as the number of
clauses satisfied goes up,

00:34:59.856 --> 00:35:02.580
the number of literals
flipped, goes down.

00:35:02.581 --> 00:35:06.170
So qualitatively, it looks
a lot like local search,

00:35:06.171 --> 00:35:08.435
some kind of an nearly.

00:35:08.436 --> 00:35:10.680
But, it's not clear
what stronger evidence

00:35:10.681 --> 00:35:12.820
would constitute
because ultimately,

00:35:12.821 --> 00:35:14.680
the algorithm is inscrutable.

00:35:14.681 --> 00:35:16.500
I can't read
the matrix instance to

00:35:16.501 --> 00:35:18.070
understand specifically
what it's doing.

00:35:18.071 --> 00:35:20.170
&gt;&gt; And we know somehow that

00:35:20.171 --> 00:35:23.265
it's also getting stuck from
the graphs and the margins.

00:35:23.266 --> 00:35:23.706
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:23.706 --> 00:35:26.820
&gt;&gt; Because it appears
to be plateauing.

00:35:26.821 --> 00:35:27.061
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:27.061 --> 00:35:31.915
&gt;&gt; So, it's not learning
enough from backtracking,

00:35:31.916 --> 00:35:36.200
enough to go search some
other space because it seems

00:35:36.201 --> 00:35:40.780
so limited in what
it's scope, somehow.

00:35:40.781 --> 00:35:41.555
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:41.556 --> 00:35:43.130
&gt;&gt; Because of the size.

00:35:43.131 --> 00:35:45.790
&gt;&gt; I think the lack of

00:35:45.791 --> 00:35:48.980
needing to do any kind of
restart during training.

00:35:48.981 --> 00:35:52.030
it's just not prepared
for such hard problems.

00:35:52.031 --> 00:35:54.260
Are you satisfied? Do you have

00:35:54.261 --> 00:35:56.005
another hypothesis
for what it's doing?

00:35:56.006 --> 00:35:57.500
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the experiment

00:35:57.501 --> 00:35:59.060
that might be interesting.

00:35:59.061 --> 00:36:00.690
If you were using
just a sequence to

00:36:00.691 --> 00:36:02.280
sequence based on strings,

00:36:02.281 --> 00:36:04.125
string encoding
of volume sensors,

00:36:04.126 --> 00:36:06.930
how would- can you approximate
your result? Is there-

00:36:06.931 --> 00:36:08.955
&gt;&gt; We implemented
a baseline and could not be

00:36:08.956 --> 00:36:13.000
50 percent on the
classification testing.

00:36:13.001 --> 00:36:14.490
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:36:14.491 --> 00:36:15.960
&gt;&gt; There's two reasons
why I think

00:36:15.961 --> 00:36:18.440
those approaches can't
get off the ground.

00:36:18.441 --> 00:36:20.720
One is that, there's
no obvious way to deliberate.

00:36:20.721 --> 00:36:22.880
I mean to deliberate,

00:36:22.881 --> 00:36:25.245
to run for a long time.

00:36:25.246 --> 00:36:27.170
A crucial part of
neuroscience is that,

00:36:27.171 --> 00:36:29.265
it's iterative, that
it can think longer.

00:36:29.266 --> 00:36:31.740
A single pass over a sequence

00:36:31.741 --> 00:36:33.470
is very unlikely to be able

00:36:33.471 --> 00:36:35.165
to solve than big,
hard problems.

00:36:35.166 --> 00:36:37.645
But also, it's just has to

00:36:37.646 --> 00:36:39.070
spend all of its time learning

00:36:39.071 --> 00:36:40.545
the invariances
that we build in.

00:36:40.546 --> 00:36:41.845
It's another thing.

00:36:41.846 --> 00:36:45.930
There's so many different
common permutation invariances,

00:36:45.931 --> 00:36:47.490
exponential number of that.

00:36:47.491 --> 00:36:48.905
&gt;&gt; But, if you were to

00:36:48.906 --> 00:36:51.300
normalize the inputs
for instance by,

00:36:51.301 --> 00:36:54.210
you sort for each
clause literals by name,

00:36:54.211 --> 00:36:55.280
and you could get part of

00:36:55.281 --> 00:36:56.475
that, and still
had this straight.

00:36:56.476 --> 00:36:57.615
So that would perhaps

00:36:57.616 --> 00:37:01.700
help test the pattern
theory so to speak,

00:37:01.701 --> 00:37:03.700
the simple fact
of recognition so

00:37:03.701 --> 00:37:06.590
far would prove powerful for
its context. I don't know.

00:37:06.591 --> 00:37:08.130
&gt;&gt; Maybe. I would
be very surprised if

00:37:08.131 --> 00:37:09.700
somebody could be
50 percent with

00:37:09.701 --> 00:37:14.760
something that didn't have
this iterative formulation,

00:37:14.761 --> 00:37:17.465
but I would be

00:37:17.466 --> 00:37:19.910
thrilled to be proved
wrong about that. Yes.

00:37:19.911 --> 00:37:22.655
&gt;&gt; So, with regards to this
picking up the patterns,

00:37:22.656 --> 00:37:24.000
you mentioned that you used

00:37:24.001 --> 00:37:25.510
this SR distribution because you

00:37:25.511 --> 00:37:27.130
don't want to pick up
some bad node terms.

00:37:27.131 --> 00:37:29.230
&gt;&gt; Yes. We designed it to
try to prevent it from,

00:37:29.231 --> 00:37:30.490
to be able to
exploit the patterns.

00:37:30.491 --> 00:37:33.750
&gt;&gt; So, did you actually do

00:37:33.751 --> 00:37:36.330
the other experiment
of completely

00:37:36.331 --> 00:37:39.860
running a
randomly-generated data,

00:37:39.861 --> 00:37:42.885
so that you see what
are the accuracies?

00:37:42.886 --> 00:37:44.885
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately, no.

00:37:44.886 --> 00:37:48.410
We've trained on
regular random set

00:37:48.411 --> 00:37:50.390
at the empirical threshold,

00:37:50.391 --> 00:37:53.320
and it does perform well also.

00:37:53.321 --> 00:37:56.370
But that's almost
as patternless as

00:37:56.371 --> 00:37:58.550
our distribution because all

00:37:58.551 --> 00:38:00.345
of the literals are of
the same statistics.

00:38:00.346 --> 00:38:02.620
Even though there's not
a single bit separated,

00:38:02.621 --> 00:38:04.090
most of the problems is at the

00:38:04.091 --> 00:38:05.185
empirical threshold
are going to be-.

00:38:05.186 --> 00:38:07.105
&gt;&gt; Which data set this is?

00:38:07.106 --> 00:38:09.450
Which data set this is?

00:38:09.451 --> 00:38:12.380
&gt;&gt; So, the data set
we generate SR event.

00:38:12.381 --> 00:38:14.760
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; It is very similar to

00:38:14.761 --> 00:38:17.950
the standard uniform
random set of problems.

00:38:17.951 --> 00:38:18.590
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:38:18.591 --> 00:38:19.990
&gt;&gt; The only difference is that,

00:38:19.991 --> 00:38:23.915
we adjust the number of
clauses specifically for

00:38:23.916 --> 00:38:25.800
each problem to find

00:38:25.801 --> 00:38:29.195
the exact threshold where
it flips from set to onset.

00:38:29.196 --> 00:38:31.230
But what you can
do is, you can just

00:38:31.231 --> 00:38:34.025
sample for a given end.

00:38:34.026 --> 00:38:37.545
That threshold is usually at
the same number of clauses,

00:38:37.546 --> 00:38:41.085
roughly 4.27 clauses
per variable ratio.

00:38:41.086 --> 00:38:42.505
So, if you just sample

00:38:42.506 --> 00:38:44.870
that number of clauses
for a fixed end,

00:38:44.871 --> 00:38:46.620
you're going to be a few flips

00:38:46.621 --> 00:38:48.870
away from flipping
my construction.

00:38:48.871 --> 00:38:52.920
We trained on that, and it
also has a similar quality.

00:38:52.921 --> 00:38:54.250
&gt;&gt; What I'm trying to say that,

00:38:54.251 --> 00:38:56.085
if your hypothesis is true,

00:38:56.086 --> 00:39:00.855
then the other way
training on that,

00:39:00.856 --> 00:39:03.815
the other data set should
do significantly better

00:39:03.816 --> 00:39:05.050
because you can pick up

00:39:05.051 --> 00:39:07.210
the patterns specific
to the data set?

00:39:07.211 --> 00:39:10.010
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, I don't
know if the random set

00:39:10.011 --> 00:39:13.565
has such patterns
close to the threshold.

00:39:13.566 --> 00:39:17.040
But, I think one of the promises
of this technique is,

00:39:17.041 --> 00:39:19.360
if there is a distribution
of set problems that

00:39:19.361 --> 00:39:22.800
has special structure that
you care about in practice,

00:39:22.801 --> 00:39:25.420
that this could
learn a less generic,

00:39:25.421 --> 00:39:26.900
more constant procedure to

00:39:26.901 --> 00:39:29.650
exploit the patterns
in that data set.

00:39:29.651 --> 00:39:33.835
Yes. But, you have to be
careful with the data set.

00:39:33.836 --> 00:39:35.940
If you can predict
satisfiability on

00:39:35.941 --> 00:39:38.160
the data set based on
superficial patterns.

00:39:38.161 --> 00:39:39.670
If you have some degenerate

00:39:39.671 --> 00:39:41.440
data set where
your satisfiable problems

00:39:41.441 --> 00:39:42.960
come from planning and

00:39:42.961 --> 00:39:45.290
your unsatisfiable problems
come from scheduling,

00:39:45.291 --> 00:39:46.830
it could potentially predict

00:39:46.831 --> 00:39:48.180
with perfect accuracy based on

00:39:48.181 --> 00:39:50.240
patterns without learning that

00:39:50.241 --> 00:39:51.710
it needs to search for
satisfying assignments.

00:39:51.711 --> 00:39:57.730
So, it wouldn't be useful.
Ideally, the usefulness

00:39:57.731 --> 00:39:59.140
of the patterns
is limited enough,

00:39:59.141 --> 00:40:00.615
that it has to learn to search,

00:40:00.616 --> 00:40:04.270
but it uses the patterns
to help search better.

00:40:04.271 --> 00:40:06.660
There's a lot of art in
the data set design I think,

00:40:06.661 --> 00:40:08.490
and we're going to see
networks that are trained on

00:40:08.491 --> 00:40:11.915
a different data set shortly
to really stress this point.

00:40:11.916 --> 00:40:16.250
Let me fast forward.

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:23.725
Okay. Let's talk about
generalizing to other domains.

00:40:23.726 --> 00:40:25.660
So, all of the test problems so

00:40:25.661 --> 00:40:27.655
far have been uniform random,

00:40:27.656 --> 00:40:29.420
which may have
its own statistical

00:40:29.421 --> 00:40:31.720
idiosyncrasies or may just,

00:40:31.721 --> 00:40:35.250
it's just inherently
uninteresting to some extent.

00:40:35.251 --> 00:40:37.890
There are an
incredible diversity

00:40:37.891 --> 00:40:39.220
of possible SAT problems.

00:40:39.221 --> 00:40:42.010
Almost hard to conceive how
the simple formalism can

00:40:42.011 --> 00:40:44.480
express such a great variety

00:40:44.481 --> 00:40:45.730
of different kinds of problems.

00:40:45.731 --> 00:40:48.075
Any problem in NP can
be reduced to SAT,

00:40:48.076 --> 00:40:49.830
and the structural and
statistical properties

00:40:49.831 --> 00:40:52.080
can really vary widely.

00:40:52.081 --> 00:40:53.540
So, an actual question is,

00:40:53.541 --> 00:40:56.360
does NeuroSAT trained
on the random problems

00:40:56.361 --> 00:40:58.280
generalize to other
domains or is it just

00:40:58.281 --> 00:41:00.885
exploiting properties
of random SAT?

00:41:00.886 --> 00:41:02.610
Now to test this, we generated

00:41:02.611 --> 00:41:04.755
a suite of diverse SAT problems.

00:41:04.756 --> 00:41:06.285
So first, we generated

00:41:06.286 --> 00:41:07.920
random graphs from a bunch of

00:41:07.921 --> 00:41:09.380
different random
graph distributions,

00:41:09.381 --> 00:41:12.755
Spare Bósi, Erdos-Renyi,
Forest Fire, and a few others.

00:41:12.756 --> 00:41:14.900
Small graphs all of
them look roughly

00:41:14.901 --> 00:41:18.080
like this about 10
Nodes, 17 Edges.

00:41:18.081 --> 00:41:22.125
For each graph, we generated
K-coloring problems,

00:41:22.126 --> 00:41:23.610
K- dominating set problems,

00:41:23.611 --> 00:41:25.785
K- clique problems
and K-cover problems.

00:41:25.786 --> 00:41:27.690
All four range of K. Where

00:41:27.691 --> 00:41:29.865
we choose K so that
of at the hard end,

00:41:29.866 --> 00:41:32.360
under 50% are satisfiable and at

00:41:32.361 --> 00:41:35.070
the easy end all of
them are satisfiable.

00:41:35.071 --> 00:41:37.510
And if you look at this graph,

00:41:37.511 --> 00:41:40.995
yeah if I remember correctly
this is a four clique,

00:41:40.996 --> 00:41:42.860
and I don't think
there's a five clique.

00:41:42.861 --> 00:41:45.680
You can look at least
with pen and paper and

00:41:45.681 --> 00:41:47.745
some thought you can

00:41:47.746 --> 00:41:49.070
solve all of these problems but

00:41:49.071 --> 00:41:50.490
they're not
perceptually obvious.

00:41:50.491 --> 00:41:53.130
Require reasoning and some kind

00:41:53.131 --> 00:41:54.480
of search even for humans.

00:41:54.481 --> 00:41:56.140
But again trivial for us,

00:41:56.141 --> 00:41:58.815
that's all we're just
because of the size.

00:41:58.816 --> 00:42:02.475
And just to really
stress the diversity,

00:42:02.476 --> 00:42:05.015
I want to show you
a few visualizations.

00:42:05.016 --> 00:42:09.320
So this is a visualization
of a problem from SR40.

00:42:09.321 --> 00:42:12.250
Here every red node
is a literal;

00:42:12.251 --> 00:42:16.505
the red edges are between a
literal and its complement.

00:42:16.506 --> 00:42:18.555
And there's a blue edge between

00:42:18.556 --> 00:42:21.525
any pair of literals that
appear in the same clause.

00:42:21.526 --> 00:42:23.360
So this is a lousy
representation

00:42:23.361 --> 00:42:25.265
because we're marginalizing
of the clauses.

00:42:25.266 --> 00:42:27.000
You can see that there's
just no structure

00:42:27.001 --> 00:42:29.855
it's just a big tangled mess.

00:42:29.856 --> 00:42:37.090
Here is a, this beast
is a problem from SR200.

00:42:37.091 --> 00:42:39.440
You can see again
there's no structure.

00:42:39.441 --> 00:42:42.825
It's just an unbelievable
tangled mess.

00:42:42.826 --> 00:42:45.530
Now just a reminder,

00:42:45.531 --> 00:42:49.290
NeuroSAT gets graphs like
this that takes time,

00:42:49.291 --> 00:42:51.200
where flipping a single

00:42:51.201 --> 00:42:54.850
edge changes whether
it's SAT or UNSAT.

00:42:54.851 --> 00:42:56.240
And it's able to
look at this and

00:42:56.241 --> 00:42:59.555
compute at least
30% of the cases,

00:42:59.556 --> 00:43:01.960
whether it's SAT
or UNSAT correctly.

00:43:01.961 --> 00:43:03.590
And actually, return
the satisfying

00:43:03.591 --> 00:43:05.245
assignment that
explains that bit.

00:43:05.246 --> 00:43:08.085
Now in contrast here's
a graph coloring problem,

00:43:08.086 --> 00:43:13.700
which has this crystalline
structure also very few edges.

00:43:13.701 --> 00:43:16.210
Here's a quick detection problem
where you've got

00:43:16.211 --> 00:43:20.030
this special pattern for
each of the five cliques.

00:43:20.031 --> 00:43:22.720
There's a dominating
SAT problem also has

00:43:22.721 --> 00:43:26.260
a very distinct structure
that is distinctly non-random,

00:43:26.261 --> 00:43:28.380
and vertex cover problem

00:43:28.381 --> 00:43:31.415
also has its own
little distinct flair.

00:43:31.416 --> 00:43:35.870
Just to stress how
different these domains are.

00:43:35.871 --> 00:43:37.590
Now let's look at the results.

00:43:37.591 --> 00:43:39.820
So in total over
this suite of problems,

00:43:39.821 --> 00:43:42.955
there were almost 5,000
satisfiable problems.

00:43:42.956 --> 00:43:45.720
Average number of
clauses is a 532.

00:43:45.721 --> 00:43:47.500
So about two and a half times

00:43:47.501 --> 00:43:50.845
bigger than the biggest problems
seen during training.

00:43:50.846 --> 00:43:53.880
And NeuroSAT solves 85% of

00:43:53.881 --> 00:43:56.350
the satisfiable
problems end to end.

00:43:56.351 --> 00:43:58.220
So again it's just trained on

00:43:58.221 --> 00:44:01.515
small random problems
for 26 iterations,

00:44:01.516 --> 00:44:04.840
and now it's solving, and
it's finding solutions to

00:44:04.841 --> 00:44:06.760
85% of problems that are

00:44:06.761 --> 00:44:10.025
bigger across a suite
of different domains.

00:44:10.026 --> 00:44:13.250
And in contrast,
the percent solved by

00:44:13.251 --> 00:44:16.785
Survey Propagation end
to end is zero percent.

00:44:16.786 --> 00:44:22.105
So Survey Propagation just
simply doesn't work on these.

00:44:22.106 --> 00:44:23.460
It usually doesn't converge,

00:44:23.461 --> 00:44:26.020
or it keeps oscillating
between one,

00:44:26.021 --> 00:44:27.905
zero and then zero, one.

00:44:27.906 --> 00:44:30.830
It's not completely a fair
comparison because Survey Prop

00:44:30.831 --> 00:44:33.120
is not designed to solve
problems end to end,

00:44:33.121 --> 00:44:34.785
it's usually used in decimation,

00:44:34.786 --> 00:44:36.690
where it just provides

00:44:36.691 --> 00:44:39.060
a heuristic for which
variables to SAT.

00:44:39.061 --> 00:44:41.260
But I keep these numbers here to

00:44:41.261 --> 00:44:43.750
stress that as far as I know,

00:44:43.751 --> 00:44:45.720
we don't know of
a message passing

00:44:45.721 --> 00:44:49.430
algorithm with qualitatively
similar behavior to NeuroSAT.

00:44:49.431 --> 00:44:50.330
So it hasn't just

00:44:50.331 --> 00:44:51.760
relearned an algorithm
that we know about,

00:44:51.761 --> 00:44:54.035
it's doing something new that we

00:44:54.036 --> 00:44:56.980
don't know how to explain. Yeah.

00:44:56.981 --> 00:45:01.235
&gt;&gt; But for the random problems,
solving problems.

00:45:01.236 --> 00:45:03.445
&gt;&gt; Yeah random problems.

00:45:03.446 --> 00:45:07.970
I haven't run it
on SRM but on run

00:45:07.971 --> 00:45:11.890
CMF so every Prop will
solve a decent fraction.

00:45:11.891 --> 00:45:13.575
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:45:13.576 --> 00:45:16.080
&gt;&gt;It doesn't work.

00:45:16.081 --> 00:45:18.020
So ironically Survey Propagation

00:45:18.021 --> 00:45:19.870
is derived
mathematically without

00:45:19.871 --> 00:45:21.300
reference to a random SAT.

00:45:21.301 --> 00:45:22.770
And doesn't generalize to

00:45:22.771 --> 00:45:25.070
non-random SAT problems as well.

00:45:25.071 --> 00:45:27.335
They are actually
trained on random SAT.

00:45:27.336 --> 00:45:28.690
But there's already
something more

00:45:28.691 --> 00:45:31.065
general that lets it transfer.

