00:57:24.111 --> 00:57:25.320
we would predict based on

00:57:25.321 --> 00:57:27.420
the clauses instead
of the literals.

00:57:27.421 --> 00:57:28.750
And then the clauses
would be more

00:57:28.751 --> 00:57:30.475
semantic and would cluster.

00:57:30.476 --> 00:57:33.890
But we don't test this
partly because during

00:57:33.891 --> 00:57:36.390
training we batch together

00:57:36.391 --> 00:57:37.830
problems that have
the same number of

00:57:37.831 --> 00:57:40.100
variables and different
numbers of clauses.

00:57:40.101 --> 00:57:43.240
So it's just, the way we set
things up it was easier to

00:57:43.241 --> 00:57:45.610
batch where we only

00:57:45.611 --> 00:57:46.850
predict from the literals since

00:57:46.851 --> 00:57:48.245
the number of
clause has changed.

00:57:48.246 --> 00:57:49.925
But in principle, with

00:57:49.926 --> 00:57:52.465
a more clever system

00:57:52.466 --> 00:57:55.500
you can predict based
on the clauses or both,

00:57:55.501 --> 00:57:58.365
and then potentially get
the unsat core directly.

00:57:58.366 --> 00:58:01.050
But it clearly has
the right semantic information.

00:58:01.051 --> 00:58:03.257
It clearly knows
about the unsat core.

00:58:03.258 --> 00:58:06.895
Let me summarize
the results so far.

00:58:06.896 --> 00:58:10.065
We train NeuroSAT as a
classifier on random problems for

00:58:10.066 --> 00:58:11.215
a few dozen iterations per

00:58:11.216 --> 00:58:14.250
problem and it works
well as a classifier.

00:58:14.251 --> 00:58:16.495
It learns to solve
the problems on its

00:58:16.496 --> 00:58:19.655
own in order to explain
the supervision bit,

00:58:19.656 --> 00:58:21.085
and we can almost always

00:58:21.086 --> 00:58:23.315
decode a solution
when it guesses SAT.

00:58:23.316 --> 00:58:27.245
It extrapolates well, so we
can run for more iterations.

00:58:27.246 --> 00:58:28.915
It can solve
much bigger problems,

00:58:28.916 --> 00:58:31.405
not with perfect reliability
but quite often,

00:58:31.406 --> 00:58:34.355
and it can solve problems
from a wide range of domains.

00:58:34.356 --> 00:58:36.515
Lastly, it learns
different algorithms

00:58:36.516 --> 00:58:38.115
depending on the training data.

00:58:38.116 --> 00:58:40.590
So in SR40,

00:58:40.591 --> 00:58:42.625
where we don't think there
are small UNSAT cores,

00:58:42.626 --> 00:58:44.665
it learns to search for
satisfying assignments

00:58:44.666 --> 00:58:47.670
and in src40 where we
apply the UNSAT cores,

00:58:47.671 --> 00:58:49.275
it learns to detect
them instead of

00:58:49.276 --> 00:58:51.660
searching for
satisfying assignments.

00:58:51.661 --> 00:58:53.395
Now, I want to share
what I think are

00:58:53.396 --> 00:58:56.730
the main lessons and
takeaways from NeuroSAT.

00:58:56.731 --> 00:58:59.790
First to answer the question
I posed in the beginning.

00:58:59.791 --> 00:59:01.685
Yes a Neural network
can learn to

00:59:01.686 --> 00:59:03.700
perform precise
logical reasoning.

00:59:03.701 --> 00:59:05.255
It might not be
perfect, it might

00:59:05.256 --> 00:59:07.530
have some extrapolation errors,

00:59:07.531 --> 00:59:09.815
but for the most part
it's clearly doing,

00:59:09.816 --> 00:59:11.015
it clearly knows when it has

00:59:11.016 --> 00:59:13.385
found a satisfying assignment.

00:59:13.386 --> 00:59:16.060
And this is a bit
sensitive property.

00:59:16.061 --> 00:59:18.265
It's sensitive to
individual bit flips in

00:59:18.266 --> 00:59:21.195
the input and so forth.

00:59:21.196 --> 00:59:23.485
Two, I think this is
the most interesting part,

00:59:23.486 --> 00:59:27.645
stochastic gradients descent
or back propagation alone,

00:59:27.646 --> 00:59:29.485
can find such programs without

00:59:29.486 --> 00:59:32.810
any discreet search even
with very weak supervision.

00:59:32.811 --> 00:59:35.550
So, it's almost amazing but

00:59:35.551 --> 00:59:37.925
the search algorithm emerges

00:59:37.926 --> 00:59:40.500
smoothly and gradually
from back propagation.

00:59:40.501 --> 00:59:42.830
There's no moment where
it learns to search.

00:59:42.831 --> 00:59:44.285
The accuracy just gradually

00:59:44.286 --> 00:59:45.595
goes up and up during training.

00:59:45.596 --> 00:59:47.690
And in by the end,

00:59:47.691 --> 00:59:51.125
it has synthesized
a search procedure

00:59:51.126 --> 00:59:53.780
but there's no moment
when it does that,

00:59:53.781 --> 00:59:56.330
there's no discrete search,
it's a smooth process.

00:59:56.331 --> 00:59:59.825
It's such an alien
computational model

00:59:59.826 --> 01:00:03.405
that it's hard to even imagine
but this is what we found.

01:00:03.406 --> 01:00:06.085
Third, message passing
architectures are,

01:00:06.086 --> 01:00:08.025
I think, surprisingly
powerful and flexible.

01:00:08.026 --> 01:00:10.565
So the same
architecture was generic

01:00:10.566 --> 01:00:12.000
enough to learn

01:00:12.001 --> 01:00:13.125
two very different algorithms

01:00:13.126 --> 01:00:14.415
depending on the training data.

01:00:14.416 --> 01:00:15.905
One is a search
procedure and one

01:00:15.906 --> 01:00:17.570
is this subgraph detector.

01:00:17.571 --> 01:00:19.925
And in fact, we have
other experiments where we train

01:00:19.926 --> 01:00:22.235
with literal level
supervision and it seems

01:00:22.236 --> 01:00:24.770
to learn an entirely
different algorithm that's

01:00:24.771 --> 01:00:26.385
more like a pattern
recognition system

01:00:26.386 --> 01:00:27.730
with no phase change,

01:00:27.731 --> 01:00:30.165
no evidence of detecting
satisfying assignments.

01:00:30.166 --> 01:00:32.405
So I think it's
a really generic architecture

01:00:32.406 --> 01:00:34.995
that can encode many different
kinds of computation.

01:00:34.996 --> 01:00:37.615
And fourth, random training
data can be useful.

01:00:37.616 --> 01:00:39.645
So conventional wisdom is that,

01:00:39.646 --> 01:00:41.120
random training data prevents

01:00:41.121 --> 01:00:43.980
learning patterns in that
if you train on random data,

01:00:43.981 --> 01:00:45.275
it's extremely unlikely to

01:00:45.276 --> 01:00:47.180
generalize to problems
that you care about.

01:00:47.181 --> 01:00:49.360
But we actually make
use of this property.

01:00:49.361 --> 01:00:51.335
We use random training
data to prevent

01:00:51.336 --> 01:00:52.575
learning patterns exactly to

01:00:52.576 --> 01:00:54.415
force it to learn an algorithm.

01:00:54.416 --> 01:00:56.185
We force it to be
more systematic.

01:00:56.186 --> 01:00:57.615
We don't think that there are

01:00:57.616 --> 01:00:59.455
enough patterns to exploit in

01:00:59.456 --> 01:01:00.895
SR40 to be able to predict

01:01:00.896 --> 01:01:03.640
that bit without
being more systematic,

01:01:03.641 --> 01:01:05.850
and we use this
to our advantage.

01:01:05.851 --> 01:01:08.355
And finally, I want to just talk

01:01:08.356 --> 01:01:10.830
about SAT solving in
practice very briefly.

01:01:10.831 --> 01:01:13.245
So modern SAT solvers as most

01:01:13.246 --> 01:01:16.080
of you know are extremely
well engineered.

01:01:16.081 --> 01:01:18.855
They can solve
real-world problems

01:01:18.856 --> 01:01:20.900
with literally millions
of variables.

01:01:20.901 --> 01:01:22.555
They're really among
the technological

01:01:22.556 --> 01:01:23.960
marvels of our time.

01:01:23.961 --> 01:01:26.530
In contrast, NeuroSAT at least

01:01:26.531 --> 01:01:29.145
as is trained in the regime
that we trained it,

01:01:29.146 --> 01:01:31.165
is vastly less reliable and

01:01:31.166 --> 01:01:34.230
efficient and scalable
than state-of-the-art.

01:01:34.231 --> 01:01:37.300
I want to make that clear.
However, we still have

01:01:37.301 --> 01:01:40.870
no idea what NeuroSAT is
and is not capable of.

01:01:40.871 --> 01:01:43.860
So, we really only trained
on toy scale so far.

01:01:43.861 --> 01:01:46.115
We train on
small random problems,

01:01:46.116 --> 01:01:47.625
for a few dozen iterations,

01:01:47.626 --> 01:01:49.965
for a few hours,
on a single GPU.

01:01:49.966 --> 01:01:52.255
And conventional wisdom
in machine learning is

01:01:52.256 --> 01:01:55.185
that scaling alone can
be extremely powerful,

01:01:55.186 --> 01:01:57.605
and it's a mistake to

01:01:57.606 --> 01:02:00.525
assume you know an upper bound
on how well your system is

01:02:00.526 --> 01:02:02.875
going to do until you really

01:02:02.876 --> 01:02:07.865
scale it up on more data
and bigger hardware.

01:02:07.866 --> 01:02:09.625
And also, there's a lot of

01:02:09.626 --> 01:02:10.715
potential room for improvement

01:02:10.716 --> 01:02:12.110
in the architecture itself.

01:02:12.111 --> 01:02:15.005
We're talking about giving
it a source of randomness as

01:02:15.006 --> 01:02:16.485
one way that might
lead synthesize

01:02:16.486 --> 01:02:18.600
a much richer search procedure.

01:02:18.601 --> 01:02:21.465
And lastly, the
hardware projections are

01:02:21.466 --> 01:02:24.090
extremely favorable for
this kind of approach.

01:02:24.091 --> 01:02:25.585
So, hardware used by

01:02:25.586 --> 01:02:27.305
traditional SAT solvers
as you all know,

01:02:27.306 --> 01:02:29.550
has stagnated and
the free lunches are over.

01:02:29.551 --> 01:02:32.015
In contrast, the Neural
network hardware renaissance

01:02:32.016 --> 01:02:34.125
is just beginning and

01:02:34.126 --> 01:02:36.775
one day a descendant
of NeuroSAT might

01:02:36.776 --> 01:02:39.745
actually be able to
beat state-of-the-art,

01:02:39.746 --> 01:02:41.215
although we have no evidence

01:02:41.216 --> 01:02:43.535
of this right now and I
don't think it's anywhere.

01:02:43.536 --> 01:02:45.585
I don't think it's
right around the corner,

01:02:45.586 --> 01:02:50.020
but 10 years out if the hardware
improves by a 1000X,

01:02:50.021 --> 01:02:52.950
I think it's possible.

01:02:52.951 --> 01:02:55.600
And that's all I
have, thank you.

01:02:55.601 --> 01:03:01.325
&gt;&gt; So, in a very abnormal knots,

01:03:01.326 --> 01:03:04.140
people have been adding
all sorts of black memories,

01:03:04.141 --> 01:03:06.040
and I mean the obvious thing
is the stack,.

01:03:06.041 --> 01:03:06.835
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:03:06.836 --> 01:03:10.425
&gt;&gt; Right? And so do you have

01:03:10.426 --> 01:03:11.950
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

01:03:11.951 --> 01:03:14.625
&gt;&gt; Converting, adding
new memories to this?

01:03:14.626 --> 01:03:17.610
&gt;&gt; So before we came up with
the unSAT core experiment,

01:03:17.611 --> 01:03:19.645
we were trying to get it to do

01:03:19.646 --> 01:03:20.815
exhaustive search and we gave it

01:03:20.816 --> 01:03:22.450
access to a Neural stack,

01:03:22.451 --> 01:03:26.945
and it didn't use, on SR40.

01:03:26.946 --> 01:03:29.895
I think we could
have come up with

01:03:29.896 --> 01:03:31.435
training data regimes where

01:03:31.436 --> 01:03:33.145
it was an incentive to use it,

01:03:33.146 --> 01:03:34.845
but SR40, it's able to

01:03:34.846 --> 01:03:36.385
explain it by
searching in a number

01:03:36.386 --> 01:03:39.795
of times steps we allow but
I think it's a good idea.

01:03:39.796 --> 01:03:42.505
Intuitively it's what
it need to be, yeah.

01:03:42.506 --> 01:03:44.590
&gt;&gt; Right, and in general I mean,

01:03:44.591 --> 01:03:47.575
I think when you're
learning programs.

01:03:47.576 --> 01:03:51.465
I mean, there's
a whole thought that will,

01:03:51.466 --> 01:03:53.595
to make the Neural net
more comprehensible,

01:03:53.596 --> 01:03:54.970
let's give it some basic

01:03:54.971 --> 01:03:57.170
data structures
we do understand,

01:03:57.171 --> 01:03:59.745
and use this but
I don't know how

01:03:59.746 --> 01:04:03.385
successful that
also control flow

01:04:03.386 --> 01:04:05.435
and program synthesis
sort of says,

01:04:05.436 --> 01:04:07.875
well let's try to use
machine learning and

01:04:07.876 --> 01:04:10.640
get the readable program
out and well,

01:04:10.641 --> 01:04:12.170
that's all they're topic.

01:04:12.171 --> 01:04:14.075
&gt;&gt; I think the algorithm
it learns could not be

01:04:14.076 --> 01:04:16.735
converted to a simple
reasonable program.

01:04:16.736 --> 01:04:18.845
&gt;&gt; Right. Most Neural
net like we don't have

01:04:18.846 --> 01:04:22.100
a chance of extracting
these things right now though.

01:04:22.101 --> 01:04:24.500
There's been some work on
getting decision trees,

01:04:24.501 --> 01:04:27.615
I don't know that, I
wouldn't follow that.

01:04:27.616 --> 01:04:29.110
&gt;&gt; I think this is,

01:04:29.111 --> 01:04:31.775
this project is
some evidence that we

01:04:31.776 --> 01:04:34.740
might still be
underestimating how

01:04:34.741 --> 01:04:36.250
powerful the algorithm that it

01:04:36.251 --> 01:04:37.885
learn the
incomprehensible ones that

01:04:37.886 --> 01:04:41.015
it learns might mean even
without being able to extract.

01:04:41.016 --> 01:04:43.085
&gt;&gt; So there's also something
interesting which is

01:04:43.086 --> 01:04:46.740
like you are doing
fix point iteration,

01:04:46.741 --> 01:04:48.990
whereas a lot of the uses of

01:04:48.991 --> 01:04:52.520
the training models
are one shop, right?

01:04:52.521 --> 01:04:55.755
And so, there's also,

01:04:55.756 --> 01:04:59.675
you're inherently
doing a fix point with

01:04:59.676 --> 01:05:02.685
no conversion guarantee but

01:05:02.686 --> 01:05:05.355
well that is also
true now, isn't it?

01:05:05.356 --> 01:05:07.535
Because in a lot of applications

01:05:07.536 --> 01:05:10.085
we just simply don't
have the time right?

01:05:10.086 --> 01:05:13.715
We need to look at the picture
and very quickly classify.

01:05:13.716 --> 01:05:15.635
There's a pedestrian
industry right?

01:05:15.636 --> 01:05:19.865
But here, you're working
in a regime where,

01:05:19.866 --> 01:05:21.595
well, that's all we can take

01:05:21.596 --> 01:05:23.075
time we can also just figure.

01:05:23.076 --> 01:05:25.105
I mean you have a model
that's iterable.

01:05:25.106 --> 01:05:27.565
&gt;&gt; Yes. Partly that
the problems are

01:05:27.566 --> 01:05:29.805
hard enough to merit
the extra computation.

01:05:29.806 --> 01:05:31.020
&gt;&gt; Right, exactly.

01:05:31.021 --> 01:05:33.655
&gt;&gt; There have been a lot of

01:05:33.656 --> 01:05:35.015
projects in the
last few years that

01:05:35.016 --> 01:05:36.795
use a similar message
passing architecture.

01:05:36.796 --> 01:05:37.226
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:37.226 --> 01:05:39.915
&gt;&gt; But they usually only run
for three or four rounds.

01:05:39.916 --> 01:05:41.475
They're doing
something juristic,

01:05:41.476 --> 01:05:43.315
not procedural like they're

01:05:43.316 --> 01:05:45.515
predicting links selling on

01:05:45.516 --> 01:05:47.450
social network graphs
or something like that,

01:05:47.451 --> 01:05:50.375
where it's not clear that
the extra computation helps,

01:05:50.376 --> 01:05:52.440
and they've shown extrapolation

01:05:52.441 --> 01:05:55.500
to more rounds improving
performance at all.

01:05:55.501 --> 01:05:59.095
I think part of this is.

01:05:59.096 --> 01:06:05.920
Going back to the unSAT
core visualization.

01:06:05.921 --> 01:06:09.290
In Neuro UNSAT,

01:06:09.291 --> 01:06:12.135
it does not make use
of more rounds at all.

01:06:12.136 --> 01:06:14.560
If you run it for
one more round,

01:06:14.561 --> 01:06:16.655
it loses this information.

01:06:16.656 --> 01:06:18.620
It doesn't even converge.

01:06:18.621 --> 01:06:21.275
It's learned this very
precise algorithms end

01:06:21.276 --> 01:06:22.825
up here at the right time.

01:06:22.826 --> 01:06:23.935
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:06:23.936 --> 01:06:27.745
&gt;&gt; And it has no reason to
preserve that information.

01:06:27.746 --> 01:06:29.715
So it's really an artifact of

01:06:29.716 --> 01:06:32.045
the training regime that it was

01:06:32.046 --> 01:06:36.305
incentivized to learn something

01:06:36.306 --> 01:06:38.075
that would extrapolate
to more rounds.

01:06:38.076 --> 01:06:39.470
And part of the reason is that,

01:06:39.471 --> 01:06:43.265
these UNSAT cores is
the same UNSAT core each time.

01:06:43.266 --> 01:06:47.525
So it's only four different
subgraph detectors it needs.

01:06:47.526 --> 01:06:49.360
It can time how long it takes.

01:06:49.361 --> 01:06:51.305
Whereas, for searching for
sense when it's timing,

01:06:51.306 --> 01:06:54.280
it's going to find
a different iteration numbers.

01:06:54.281 --> 01:06:56.855
So it needs to learn to
preserve that information.

01:06:56.856 --> 01:07:00.395
So it learns to
converge around those.

01:07:00.396 --> 01:07:03.460
Even though it's not
trained for more iterations,

01:07:03.461 --> 01:07:06.290
it least learns convergence.

01:07:06.291 --> 01:07:08.425
Because sometimes it's
going to find it on

01:07:08.426 --> 01:07:11.090
iteration five,
sometimes iteration 15,

01:07:11.091 --> 01:07:13.215
and it's penalized
very heavily for

01:07:13.216 --> 01:07:16.835
losing the solution
that it counts.

01:07:16.836 --> 01:07:19.045
&gt;&gt; Okay, cool. So, if you threw

01:07:19.046 --> 01:07:21.020
randomness into this
and parallelize,

01:07:21.021 --> 01:07:23.325
you might bump up these curves.

01:07:23.326 --> 01:07:25.185
A bit that you showed for

01:07:25.186 --> 01:07:28.225
the larger instances
that's purely to be used.

01:07:28.226 --> 01:07:29.615
&gt;&gt; I think for us, I start to

01:07:29.616 --> 01:07:31.400
wonder we can get 100% accuracy.

01:07:31.401 --> 01:07:32.590
&gt;&gt; Really?

01:07:32.591 --> 01:07:34.905
It's randomization,
just adding randomly.

01:07:34.906 --> 01:07:38.065
&gt;&gt; And just training
on sr100, SR200.

01:07:38.066 --> 01:07:40.050
&gt;&gt; Oh sure, sure, you're
doing the training there.

01:07:40.051 --> 01:07:40.376
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:07:40.376 --> 01:07:42.835
&gt;&gt; But it would be
nice to just train on

01:07:42.836 --> 01:07:44.755
smaller instances
and then probably

01:07:44.756 --> 01:07:47.630
just have some
technique to boost.

01:07:47.631 --> 01:07:50.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So I don't know how,

01:07:50.731 --> 01:07:55.375
I think empirically, 40 is
not hard enough to make,

01:07:55.376 --> 01:07:58.455
it needs to learn something
that can do restart.

01:07:58.456 --> 01:08:01.645
Maybe at 100 it can
learn something really

01:08:01.646 --> 01:08:04.895
general that would extrapolate
to much bigger problems.

01:08:04.896 --> 01:08:06.405
I don't think you
need to train on

01:08:06.406 --> 01:08:08.260
exactly the size you
are going to test on.

01:08:08.261 --> 01:08:10.945
But you need to train
on something that

01:08:10.946 --> 01:08:16.080
is qualitatively in some
similar ballpark of difficulty.

01:08:16.081 --> 01:08:18.875
Or at least it's hard
enough to require

01:08:18.876 --> 01:08:20.700
you to learn the tricks you need

01:08:20.701 --> 01:08:24.300
to solve the bigger problem.

01:08:24.301 --> 01:08:26.055
One of the things I really
want to stress is just how

01:08:26.056 --> 01:08:28.970
sensitive what it learns
is to the training data.

01:08:28.971 --> 01:08:31.855
It will learn essentially
the simplest thing

01:08:31.856 --> 01:08:34.275
that lets it predict well
on the training data,

01:08:34.276 --> 01:08:35.855
and if that simple thing is

01:08:35.856 --> 01:08:37.585
not what you need to
solve the harder problems,

01:08:37.586 --> 01:08:39.120
